*Definition.* For a finite ring $R$ and integers $t, m >= 1$, let $egz(t, R, m)$ be the least $l$
such that every sequence $S$ of length $l$ over $R$ has a subsequence $S'$ of length $t$ with
$e_m (S') = 0$. In the group case,
$ D(G) defeq min_(t >= 1) egz(t, G, 1). $

*Theorem (Erdős–Ginzburg–Ziv).* For every $n >= 1$,
$ egz(n, Zb_n, 1) = 2n - 1. $

For a prime $p$ we have $carac(Zb_p) = p$, and $a^p equiv a pmod(p)$ for every $a in Zb$. Split a
sequence over $Zb_2$ as $S = S_0 cupdot S_1$ by value. Then
$
  e_m (S) = binom(|S_1|, m)
  equals(by: "Lucas") product_(i=0)^k binom(s_i, m_i)
  pmod(2).
$

Finally, in an inner-product space, $Re inner(u, v) <= norm(u) norm(v)$ and
$norm(u)^2 = inner(u, u)$.
